Postdoc:  ```PLAN
Title: Experiment for Maximizing Algorithmic Creativity via Next-Token and Multi-Token/Diffusion Approaches

Objective:
Design a minimal yet innovative experimental framework to compare traditional next-token prediction (NTP) with multi-token teacherless training (MTP) and a discrete diffusion baseline (SEDD, absorb variant) on minimal open-ended algorithmic tasks (“Sibling Discovery”, “Triangle Discovery”, “Circle Construction”, “Line Construction”). The goal is to evaluate each method’s ability to generate outputs that are coherent, original, and diverse while reducing memorization.

Overview:
1. Dataset:
   • Create synthetic benchmark datasets for the four tasks defined by coherence functions and uniqueness/originality criteria.
   • Each task will have well-defined input prompts and a set of valid “creative” responses. Optionally, augment with established summarization datasets (e.g., XSUM, CNN/DailyMail) to analyze diversity-quality tradeoffs via Self-BLEU vs ROUGE scores.

2. Models:
   • NTP: A Transformer trained with teacher forcing (standard next-token prediction).
   • MTP: A Transformer trained with a teacherless paradigm, predicting all response tokens in parallel given the prompt and dummy response inputs.
   • Discrete Diffusion (SEDD): A small-parameter discrete diffusion model (absorb variant) that uses score-entropy diffusion steps to generate multi-token outputs, matching roughly the Transformer’s parameter count.

3. Randomness Elicitation Techniques:
   • Sampling modes: Compare temperature scaling (and optionally top-k/top-p sampling) at output with seed-conditioning (providing random input prefixes) paired with deterministic decoding.
   • Systematic ablation studies: Vary seed length, sampling temperature, top-k top-p on/off, and test alternate ordering of task tokens (e.g., reversing token order in Sibling Discovery).

4. Training and Implementation Details:
   • For each method, train on the synthetic tasks. Ensure the dataset variety allows for meaningful assessment of creative generation.
   • Use standard Transformer architectures (with matched small parameter count for fair comparison) for NTP and MTP. For SEDD, implement a discrete diffusion framework that uses iterative refinement to generate blocks of tokens.
   • Ensure all models share similar training dataset sizes and complexity to isolate differences that arise from training paradigms and decoding strategies.

5. Evaluation Metrics:
   • Primary Metric – Algorithmic Creativity (ˆcrN): Fraction of generated samples that are coherent (pass task-specific logical checks), unique (diverse and not a verbatim training copy), and original.
   • Component Metrics: Coherence rate (task-specific correctness), diversity (unique token count divided by total tokens generated), and memorization rate.
   • Optional: For summarization adaptation, measure Self-BLEU and ROUGE scores to study diversity-quality tradeoffs under NTP vs MTP vs diffusion approaches.

6. Experiment Procedure:
   a. Train three models (NTP, MTP, SEDD) on the generated tasks.
   b. For each model, generate responses under varied randomness conditions:
      - Standard temperature sampling (with/without top-k/top-p) vs deterministic seed-conditioned decoding.
   c. Execute controlled ablations: vary seed lengths, temperature, top-k settings, task token ordering, dataset size, and task complexity parameters (e.g., triangle density for Triangle Discovery, size parameters for Circle/Line tasks).
   d. Record metrics for each configuration and perform comparative analyses.
   e. Optionally, extend experiments to summarization tasks to measure tradeoffs in self-similarity and summarization quality.

7. Expected Contributions:
   • Demonstrate that MTP and diffusion (SEDD) models offer improved algorithmic creativity over traditional NTP.
   • Validate that seed-conditioning can induce diversity on par with stochastic sampling methods.
   • Provide evidence that MTP reduces memorization while seed-conditioning enhances output diversity.
   • Offer a systematic ablation framework that highlights how model and decoding parameter choices affect creativity in minimally defined algorithmic tasks.

Timeline:
• Week 1–2: Dataset synthesis and baseline implementation.
• Week 3–5: Implementation and training of NTP and MTP paradigms.
• Week 6–7: Development and integration of the discrete diffusion (SEDD) baseline.
• Week 8–10: Run controlled experiments and ablations; analyze outcomes.
• Week 11: Draft report summarizing experimental findings.

This plan provides a clear pathway to integrate recent advances in prompt engineering and multi-token reasoning methods to push the boundaries of algorithmic creativity. It leverages the insights from CreTok, Top-H Decoding, d2, and SDAR studies, offering a novel experimental comparison of token-level vs multi-token diffusion-based strategies for minimal open-ended tasks.
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
